    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Calcineurin is a conserved Ca2+/calmodulin-activated phosphatase that plays a crucial role in many signaling pathways in both mammals and yeast. In mammals, calcineurin plays a role in T-cell activation, cardiac hypertrophy, and neuronal development. The widely used FDA-approved immunosuppressants cyclosporin A and FK506 both inhibit calcineurin, yet are associated with various side effects. Calcineurin is a heterodimer consisting of a catalytic A subunit (CNA) and a regulatory B subunit (CNB). When Ca2+ binds to the B subunit and the Ca2+-calmodulin complex binds to the A subunit, a conformational change causes the auto-inhibitory domain (AID) to be removed from the active site, allowing access to substrates. While some substrate interaction sites have been identified and well characterized, there is evidence that more sites may exist. A bioinformatics approach was used to identify conserved regions on the surface of calcineurin.\r\n \r\nThis research aims to elucidate the function of one such conserved surface region. Site-directed mutagenesis was used to replace conserved amino acids on CNA to alanine, a non-interacting amino acid, creating two mutants: CNA(N287A) and CNA(C292A, F294A). Calcineurin’s high degree of evolutionary conservation permits the study of the protein in S. cerevisiae (baker’s yeast). In vivo and in vitro assays were used to measure calcineurin-dependent transcription and dephosphorylation. None of the mutations led to decreased protein expression compared to wild-type calcineurin. Results showed that CNA(N287A) has increased activity in vivo, while decreased activity in vitro, and CNA(C292A, F294A) has decreased activity both in vivo and in vitro. While it is currently not understood why there is a discrepancy between the results for CNA(N287A), this project shows that the conserved region defined by these residues allosterically affects enzymatic activity. Experiments are already underway to determine if this region is also implicated in calcineurin activation through an AID-dependent mechanism.\r\n\r\nUnderstanding how calcineurin is activated and regulated provides valuable information for studying the various pathways in which it is a part of. Furthermore, learning more about calcineurin’s structure provides vital information for better drug design.",
